theory.intro:
An obtuse angle $\theta$, with $90^{\circ}<\theta<180^{\circ}$, lies in the second quadrant. Its ratios equal those of its acute supplement $180^{\circ}-\theta$, with the second-quadrant sign attached: sine stays positive, cosine and tangent turn negative.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (X) at (1.2,0);
\coordinate (Xn) at (-1.2,0);
\coordinate (P) at (145:2.4);
\coordinate (Q) at (35:2.4);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (O) circle (2.4);
\draw[thick, blue] (O)--(P);
\draw[thick, gray, dashed] (O)--(Q);
\fill (P) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[gray] (Q) circle (1.6pt);
\node[above left] at (P) {$P$};
\node[above right] at (Q) {$P'$};
\node[below right] at (O) {$O$};
\pic[draw, blue, "$\theta$", angle radius=1.35cm, angle eccentricity=1.4] {angle=X--O--P};
\pic[draw, red, "$A$", angle radius=0.65cm, angle eccentricity=2.3] {angle=P--O--Xn};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
$\begin{aligned}A&=180^{\circ}-128^{\circ}\\&=52^{\circ}\end{aligned}$

The point lies in Q2, where the $y$-coordinate is positive, so sine is positive.

